  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The dataset is restricted to 539 homeowners, excluding renters who may experience different psychosocial dynamics regarding housing market status.",
      "what": "Serial mediation analysis of 539 homeowners across seven South Korean metropolitan cities shows that higher neighborhood housing price rank reduces depressive symptoms by elevating subjective social status and lowering status anxiety. Absolute housing prices exhibit no statistically significant direct or indirect association with depressive symptoms.",
      "why": "Mental well-being relates to relative position within a citywide housing hierarchy rather than absolute housing wealth, establishing positional status as a distinct psychosocial driver of urban health inequality."
    }
